{
 "id": "ch32",
 "intro": [
  {
   "blocks": [
    {
     "c": [
      {
       "c": [
        "How to take this mock exam."
       ],
       "t": "b"
      },
      " This is the ",
      {
       "c": [
        "hardest"
       ],
       "t": "i"
      },
      " of the three mock exams. It tests edge cases, combinations of features, exact default values and precedence rules. If you pass this one, the real exam will feel comfortable."
     ],
     "t": "p"
    },
    {
     "items": [
      {
       "blocks": [
        {
         "c": [
          {
           "c": [
            "Time:"
           ],
           "t": "b"
          },
          " 120 minutes for 65 questions. Use a timer. Stop when the time is over."
         ],
         "t": "p"
        }
       ]
      },
      {
       "blocks": [
        {
         "c": [
          {
           "c": [
            "No notes:"
           ],
           "t": "b"
          },
          " no book, no documentation, no terminal. Work from memory, like in the real exam."
         ],
         "t": "p"
        }
       ]
      },
      {
       "blocks": [
        {
         "c": [
          {
           "c": [
            "Answer everything:"
           ],
           "t": "b"
          },
          " a wrong answer costs nothing, so never leave a question empty."
         ],
         "t": "p"
        }
       ]
      },
      {
       "blocks": [
        {
         "c": [
          {
           "c": [
            "DOMC questions:"
           ],
           "t": "b"
          },
          " cover the options with a sheet of paper. Uncover ",
          {
           "c": [
            "one"
           ],
           "t": "i"
          },
          " option, write YES or NO for it, then uncover the next one. Never go back to change an option you already judged."
         ],
         "t": "p"
        }
       ]
      },
      {
       "blocks": [
        {
         "c": [
          {
           "c": [
            "Multiple select:"
           ],
           "t": "b"
          },
          " choose exactly the number of options asked. ",
          {
           "c": [
            "Matching"
           ],
           "t": "b"
          },
          " and ",
          {
           "c": [
            "Build list"
           ],
           "t": "b"
          },
          ": all items must be right to get the point."
         ],
         "t": "p"
        }
       ]
      },
      {
       "blocks": [
        {
         "c": [
          "Write your answers on the answer sheet at the end of this chapter. Then correct yourself with ",
          {
           "href": "/learn/ch33/",
           "label": "ch:mock3-answers",
           "t": "ref",
           "text": "Chapter 33"
          },
          ", which also tells you what to review for every question."
         ],
         "t": "p"
        }
       ]
      }
     ],
     "kind": "ul",
     "t": "list"
    },
    {
     "c": [
      {
       "c": [
        "The scenario."
       ],
       "t": "b"
      },
      " StreamBox is a video streaming company. Its dbt project ",
      {
       "t": "code",
       "v": "streambox"
      },
      " reads raw tables in the schemas ",
      {
       "t": "code",
       "v": "raw_app"
      },
      " (",
      {
       "t": "code",
       "v": "subscriptions"
      },
      ", ",
      {
       "t": "code",
       "v": "devices"
      },
      "), ",
      {
       "t": "code",
       "v": "raw_billing"
      },
      " (",
      {
       "t": "code",
       "v": "invoices_v2"
      },
      ", ",
      {
       "t": "code",
       "v": "refunds"
      },
      ", ",
      {
       "t": "code",
       "v": "payouts"
      },
      ") and ",
      {
       "t": "code",
       "v": "raw_events"
      },
      " (",
      {
       "t": "code",
       "v": "playback_events"
      },
      "). Developers work on DuckDB with the target ",
      {
       "t": "code",
       "v": "dev"
      },
      " (schema ",
      {
       "t": "code",
       "v": "dbt_adnane"
      },
      "); production runs with the target ",
      {
       "t": "code",
       "v": "prod"
      },
      " (schema ",
      {
       "t": "code",
       "v": "analytics"
      },
      "). The database is called ",
      {
       "t": "code",
       "v": "streambox"
      },
      ". Unless a question says otherwise, all defaults of dbt Core 1.11 apply."
     ],
     "t": "p"
    }
   ],
